      "description": "International Asian Studies covers a broad range of topics and different regional and cultural contexts. The focus of the course varies according to the area of expertise of the instructor. The instructor will design lectures, tutorials and fieldtrips around a special topic(s) relevant to Hong Kong, China, the Asia region. Drawing on the background, experiences and interests of the students, the course will consider the topics from different cultural, geographical and societal perspectives. The course is set at an elementary level and encourages students to cultivate interest and explore the subject knowledge in relation to Asian studies from a global perspective.",
      "enrollment_requirement": "",
      "course_attributes": "",
      "academic_career": "Undergraduate",
      "grading_basis": "Graded",
      "component": "Discussion\nField Studies/Field-trip\nLecture",
      "campus": "Main Campus",
      "academic_group": "International Asian Studies PC",
      "academic_org": "International Asian Studies PC",
      "learning_outcomes": "Upon successful completion of this course, students should be able to:\n\n1. Deepen their understanding of historical, social, political and cultural issues pertaining to Asian societies.\n2. Conduct investigation on issues by drawing on and interpreting data from multiple sources.\n3. Critically and empirically analyze contemporary issues from global perspectives.\n4. Demonstrate effective communication with people from different backgrounds.\n5. Develop and enhance skills needed for interdisciplinary research and academic discourse.",
      "assessment_types": {
        "Essays": "40",
        "Participation": "20",
        "Report": "40"
      }
    },
