      "description": "This is a project-based course in which students will be grouped into different teams. Members of each group shall come from different disciplines in at least two different faculties and comprised of both international and local students as far as possible.\nEach member of the research group should approach the topic from their own discipline and a comparative analysis should be made of varying Chinese and Western perspectives. By doing so, each student will gain an all-round knowledge and diverse perspective of the topic.",
      "enrollment_requirement": "Not for students who have taken GECC3130, 3230, 3430 or 4130.",
      "course_attributes": "",
      "academic_career": "Undergraduate",
      "grading_basis": "Graded",
      "component": "Lecture\nProject\nSeminar",
      "campus": "Main Campus",
      "academic_group": "International Asian Studies PC",
      "academic_org": "International Asian Studies PC",
      "learning_outcomes": "After completing this course, students should be able to:\n\n1. Deepen their understanding of current affairs and cultural issues pertaining to Chinese and Asian societies.\n2. Possess the skills to conduct an effective and collaborative research.\n3. Demonstrate effective cross-cultural exchange with both international and local students.\n4. Develop and enhance the skills of inter-disciplinary research and discussion.",
      "assessment_types": {
        "Discussion": "20",
        "Presentation": "30",
        "Report": "50"
      }
